# ADR-2607161956: kotoba-lang/bytes — shared byte-vector/SHA-1 primitives extracted from kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn (Phase 1 of a net/turn/dtn shared-lib consolidation)

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-07-16
**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki

## Problem

After the `kotoba-lang/dtn` gap-closing series (ADR-2607161743 through
ADR-2607161951), the user directed: *「net, turn をちゃんと実装, 共有libを設計して
効率化, 依存効率化」* — properly implement `kotoba-lang/net` and
`kotoba-lang/turn`'s real I/O (both were, like `dtn` before
ADR-2607161817, protocol-semantics-only with zero network I/O), and do so
by designing a shared library rather than duplicating plumbing across
three repos.

A quick read-only audit of both repos before writing any code surfaced two
facts that reshaped the plan:

1. Neither `kotoba-lang/net` (`kotoba.net.gossip`/`kotoba.net.bitswap` —
   pure fanout/dedup/want-have semantics) nor `kotoba-lang/turn` (STUN
   codec + credential mint/verify) has ANY real socket I/O — both
   explicitly name \"actual network I/O\", \"NAT traversal\", and \"peer
   discovery\" as out of scope in their own READMEs, the same honest-scoping
   discipline `dtn` itself used before ADR-2607161817. Building real I/O
   for either is a genuinely new build, not a \"wire dtn to an existing
   capability\" integration — the earlier gap inventory had understated
   this.
2. `kotoba-lang/turn` already has exactly the class of shared primitive
   `dtn`'s TCP transport (ADR-2607161817) currently reimplements ad-hoc:
   `kotoba.turn.bytes` (portable byte-vector codecs — u16/u32 BE, XOR,
   padding, base64) and `kotoba.turn.sha1` (pure SHA-1/HMAC-SHA1, no
   platform crypto API, byte-identical on JVM and ClojureScript). Neither
   is actually TURN/STUN-specific; they were simply built there first.

This ADR is Phase 1 of the resulting plan: extract the generic primitives
before building new I/O on top of anything, so `dtn`'s future transport
refactor (Phase 2), `turn`'s real relay I/O (Phase 3), and `net`'s real
gossip I/O (Phase 4) all draw from one shared foundation instead of each
reinventing byte-level plumbing.

## Decision

### Decision 1: extract, don't duplicate — `kotoba.turn.bytes`/`kotoba.turn.sha1` move to a new `kotoba-lang/bytes` repo

`src/kotoba/bytes.cljc` (was `kotoba.turn.bytes`) and
`src/kotoba/bytes/sha1.cljc` (was `kotoba.turn.sha1`) are exact
move-and-rename extractions — same functions, same docstrings, same
implementations, verified byte-for-byte identical except for the `ns` and
`require` lines. `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn` no longer owns this code; it
depends on `kotoba-lang/bytes` via `:local/root \"../bytes\"`, the same
sibling-checkout dependency pattern every other `kotoba-lang` library in
this workspace already uses.

### Decision 2: this is a mechanical refactor, verified by identical behavior, not a rewrite

The correctness bar for this ADR is narrow and strict: `turn`'s actual
STUN/credential behavior must be provably unchanged. Before the refactor,
`turn` had 24 deftest / 53 assertions total (bytes + sha1 + credential +
stun combined) and 0 lint issues. After: `turn` itself has 22 deftest / 43
assertions (credential's 13/23 and stun's 9/20, untouched) and the new
`bytes` repo has the 2/10 that moved with `sha1_test`. 43 + 10 = 53 — the
full assertion count is conserved across the split, not merely
\"approximately the same.\" Both repos independently lint clean.

### Decision 3: `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn` is this repo's actual current name — a rename this ADR discovered mid-flight, not caused

While registering this work in the manifest, `gh api repos/kotoba-lang/turn`
resolved to `full_name: \"kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn\"` — the repo was renamed
at some point before this ADR series began; `kotoba-lang/turn` is now only
a GitHub redirect alias (clones/pushes against the old name still work via
redirect, which is how the implementing agent's session used it without
noticing). `manifest/west.yml` already had it correctly registered as
`org-ietf-turn` at `orgs/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn` before this ADR — this
ADR's own pin advance for it uses the correct current name. Recorded here
so future ADRs in this series reference `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn`, not
the stale `kotoba-lang/turn` alias used informally in the Problem section
above (quoted there only because that's the name used when the user's
directive was issued and the initial research was performed).

### Decision 4: `xor-bytes`/`pad-right`/`base64-decode`/`constant-time-eq` have no direct unit tests — a pre-existing gap, surfaced not created

The implementing agent found, while trying to move a \"bytes test file\"
that turned out not to exist as a standalone unit: `kotoba.turn.bytes` was
only ever exercised indirectly (e.g. via `utf8-encode` inside other
modules' tests), never directly. This gap predates this extraction — it is
recorded here rather than silently carried forward unremarked, matching
this workspace's practice of naming coverage gaps explicitly (see
ADR-2607161852/2607161922's own \"known limitation\" sections). Direct unit
tests for these functions are additive future work, not required to land
this extraction (the functions' actual behavior is still exercised,
indirectly, by every consumer's existing test).

## Verification

- `kotoba-lang/bytes`: `clojure -M:test` → 2 tests / 10 assertions, 0 failures/errors (exactly `turn`'s original `sha1_test.clj`, moved). `clojure -M:lint` → 0 errors, 0 warnings. Pushed to `github.com/kotoba-lang/bytes` (public), commit `263bb0ded79aefb4ab181e659d8b23419b0b9334`. Independently re-verified by the orchestrating session from a cold clone — identical results.
- `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn`: baseline (before) 24 tests / 53 assertions, 0 lint issues. After: `clojure -M:test` → 22 tests / 43 assertions, 0 failures/errors (credential 13/23 + stun 9/20, both unchanged from baseline). `clojure -M:lint` → 0 errors, 0 warnings. `deps.edn` confirmed depending on `io.github.kotoba-lang/bytes {:local/root \"../bytes\"}`; no stray `bytes.cljc`/`sha1.cljc` files left in `src/`. Pushed, commit `ce6cee2876f4489770e1663d05abd4986fe827f6`. Independently re-verified from a cold clone via the correct canonical URL (`github.com/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn`) — identical results.
- `manifest/repos.edn`/`manifest/west.yml`: `bytes` registered via `--entry bytes,org-ietf-turn` (new entry, `:extra-projects` addition), `org-ietf-turn`'s pin advanced (`53643eb61058→ce6cee2876f4`), `verify-west-pins: 2 件の pin 変更をすべて検証 OK` — 5 unrelated pending pin changes elsewhere in the tree correctly left untouched by the scoped `--entry` invocation. Landed via an isolated sibling-path worktree + GitHub API server-side merge, matching every prior ADR in this series.

## Consequences

- `kotoba-lang` now has one canonical home for portable byte-vector codecs
  and pure SHA-1/HMAC-SHA1 — `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn`'s own STUN/credential
  work depends on it rather than owning it, and `dtn`'s Phase 2 transport
  refactor (next) and `net`'s Phase 4 real-I/O work (later) can depend on
  the same repo instead of each re-implementing u16/u32 codecs or a pure
  SHA-1.
- The \"net/turn ready-made building blocks\" assumption implicit in
  ADR-2607161743/ADR-2607161922's gap inventories (which named
  `kotoba-lang/net`/`kotoba-lang/turn` as composition points for peer
  discovery/NAT traversal as if the capability already existed) is
  corrected here: both need real, substantial new I/O work of their own
  before that composition is meaningful — this ADR does not perform that
  composition, only lays a shared foundation two future phases will build
  on.
- `xor-bytes`/`pad-right`/`base64-decode`/`constant-time-eq` remain without
  direct unit tests (Decision 4) — a known, pre-existing, now-explicitly-
  recorded gap, not a regression introduced by this extraction.
- The `kotoba-lang/turn` → `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn` rename (Decision 3)
  is now recorded in this ADR series; future work here should use the
  current name.

## Alternatives considered

- **Leaving `kotoba.turn.bytes`/`kotoba.turn.sha1` inside `turn` and having
  `dtn`'s (and future `net`'s) transport work depend directly on `turn`**
  Rejected: `turn` is a STUN/TURN-specific protocol library; making
  unrelated repos depend on it just to reach two generic utility
  namespaces would be a confusing, backwards dependency direction — the
  generic primitives should be the shared root, not a side effect of
  depending on one specific protocol implementation.
- **Copy-pasting `kotoba.turn.bytes`/`kotoba.turn.sha1` into `dtn` instead
  of extracting a shared repo.** Rejected: this is precisely the
  duplication the user's \"依存効率化\" directive asked to avoid — two
  divergence-prone copies of the same byte-codec logic instead of one.
- **Rewriting/\"improving\" the algorithms while moving them.** Rejected:
  this ADR's whole verification story depends on the move being provably
  behavior-preserving (Decision 2's conserved assertion count); improving
  the code, if warranted, is a separate, independently-reviewable future
  change to `kotoba-lang/bytes` once it exists on its own.

